Do not read the Android package list when nothing asks for it.

Starting a TUN listener on Android goes through `buildAndroidRules`, which
unconditionally starts sing-tun's package manager. The manager reads
`/data/system/packages.xml` -- a file SELinux keeps readable by `system` only
since Android 11. From an ordinary app (`untrusted_app`) the read fails with
EACCES, `buildAndroidRules` returns the error, and `sing_tun.New` never gets
called:

    Start TUN listening error: build android rules: read packages list:
    open /data/system/packages.xml: permission denied

The core survives that -- `ReCreateTun` logs the error, clears `tun.enable` and
carries on -- so everything else looks healthy: the local SOCKS inbound is up,
the proxy dials fine, the app reports "connected". Only nobody ever reads the
tun device, so not a single packet moves.

Upstream sidesteps this with the `cmfa` build tag: ClashMetaForAndroid runs
mihomo as a library and feeds it the package list from Java. That tag is not an
option here -- it also swaps the `system` DNS client for one that is fed
through a Java bridge we do not have, disables the loopback detector and puts
the RESTful API into embed mode. We run the core as a plain executable, so the
non-cmfa build is the right one; it just must not die on a list it does not
need.

And it genuinely does not need it. The list serves exactly one purpose:
translating `include-package` / `exclude-package` into UID ranges. This app
never sets either -- per-app routing on Android is done by VpnService
(`addAllowedApplication` / `addDisallowedApplication`), which is the only thing
that works without privileges anyway. `BuildAndroidRules` touches the manager
only inside those two branches, so with both empty it can be handed a nil one;
the android-user handling it still performs (scanning `/data/user`) needs no
manager.

Keep the guard in step with the fields `BuildAndroidRules` resolves through the
package manager: should sing-tun learn a third package-shaped option, it has to
be listed here too, or that option would silently do nothing.

diff --git a/listener/sing_tun/server_android.go b/listener/sing_tun/server_android.go
--- a/listener/sing_tun/server_android.go
+++ b/listener/sing_tun/server_android.go
@@ -47,6 +47,18 @@
 }

 func (l *Listener) buildAndroidRules(tunOptions *tun.Options) error {
+	// keqdroid: the package manager exists to turn package names into UIDs.
+	// With no package rules there is nothing to turn, and reading the list is
+	// not permitted for an ordinary app at all: SELinux keeps
+	// /data/system/packages.xml readable by system only, so Start() fails with
+	// EACCES and takes the whole TUN listener down with it. A nil manager is
+	// safe here -- BuildAndroidRules only dereferences it inside the
+	// IncludePackage/ExcludePackage branches, and the android-user handling it
+	// still performs needs no manager at all.
+	if len(tunOptions.IncludePackage) == 0 && len(tunOptions.ExcludePackage) == 0 {
+		tunOptions.BuildAndroidRules(nil, l.handler)
+		return nil
+	}
 	packageManager, err := getPackageManager()
 	if err != nil {
 		return err
